s 9 digits: 9 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), etc. The second number is 507,199,088 which is also 9 digits: 5 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 7 (millions), etc. Wait, so when adding, the billions place would be 9 (from first number's hundred millions place) plus 5 (from second number's hundred millions place)? Wait no, actually, each digit's place should be properly aligned. Let me make sure I'm aligning them correctly.\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas to see:\n\nFirst number: 999,474,677 (which is 999 million, 474 thousand, 677)\nSecond number: 507,199,088 (507 million, 199 thousand, 88)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nBillions place: 0 (since both are less than a billion, so the highest is hundred millions place). Wait, actually, 999 million is less than a billion (which is 1,000 million). So the highest digit is the hundred millions place.\n\nWait, maybe I need to think in terms of each digit's position. Let me write them out digit by digit:\n\nFirst number (999,474,677):\n- Billions place: 0 (since it's less than a billion)\n- Hundred millions: 9\n- Ten millions: 9\n- Millions: 9\n- Hundred thousands: 4\n- Ten thousands: 7\n- Thousands: 4\n- Hundreds: 6\n- Tens: 7\n- Ones: 7\n\nSecond number (507,199,088):\n- Billions place: 0\n- Hundred millions:5\n- Ten millions:0\n- Millions:7\n- Hundred thousands:1\n- Ten thousands:9\n- Thousands:9\n- Hundreds:0\n- Tens:8\n- Ones:8\n\nWait, actually, let me confirm the positions correctly. Let's break down each number digit by digit from right to left (ones to billions):\n\nFirst number: 999,474,677\n\nStarting from the right:\nPosition 1 (ones):7\nPosition 2 (tens):7\nPosition3 (hundreds):6\nPosition4 (thousands):4\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):4\nPosition7 (millions):9\nPosition8 (ten millions):9\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9\nPosition10 (billions):0 (since it's a 9-digit number)\n\nSecond number:507,199,088\n\nPosition1 (ones):8\nPosition2 (tens):8\nPosition3 (hundreds):0\nPosition4 (thousands):9\nPosition5 (ten thousands):9\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1\nPosition7 (millions):7\nPosition8 (ten millions):0\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5\nPosition10 (billions):0 (since it's a 9-digit number)\n\nNow adding each position step by step, starting from the rightmost (ones place):\n\nPosition1 (ones): 7 + 8 =15 → write 5, carryover 1\nPosition2 (tens):7 +8 +1 (carry) =16 → write 6, carryover 1. Wait wait, earlier I thought the tens place was 7+0 but maybe I made a mistake here. Let me redo the positions carefully.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the digit positions earlier. Let me re-express each number as digits in each place properly.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically aligned:\n\nFirst number:  9 9 9 4 7 4 6 7 7\n               (hundred millions, ten millions, millions, hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens, ones?)\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to separate each digit with commas as per standard:\n\n999,474,677 is:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):\n\n1 (ones):7\n\n2 (tens):7\n\n3 (hundreds):6\n\n4 (thousands):4\n\n5 (ten thousands):7\n\n6 (hundred thousands):4\n\n7 (millions):9\n\n8 (ten millions):9\n\n9 (hundred millions):9\n\n10 (billions):0\n\nSecond number:507,199,088 is:\n\n1 (ones):8\n\n2 (tens):8\n\n3 (hundreds):0\n\n4 (thousands):9\n\n5 (ten thousands):9\n\n6 (hundred thousands):1\n\n7 (millions):7\n\n8 (ten millions):0\n\n9 (hundred millions):5\n\n10 (billions):0\n\nSo adding each position:\n\nStarting from position 1 (ones):\n\n7 +8 =15 → digit 5, carryover 1\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n7 (from first) +8 (from second) +1 (carry) = 16 → digit 6, carryover1\n\nWait, that's different from my first calculation. Previously I thought the second number's tens digit was 0, but no, in the second number, the tens place is 8 (since the last two digits are 88). So the tens digit is 8. So that's where I messed up earlier. I must have made a mistake in the initial step.\n\nSo correcting that:\n\nPosition