its are:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\n9 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n9 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n0 (thousands),\n\n1 (hundreds),\n\n4 (tens),\n\n6 (ones).\n\nWhich is 1,190,990,146. But when adding via the alternative method, I must have made an error.\n\nWait let's compute 389,136,634 +807,826,512 numerically:\n\n389,136,634\n\n+807,826,512\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart with 389,136,634 +800,000,000 =1,189,136,634.\n\nThen add 7,826,512:\n\n1,189,136,634 +7,000,000 =1,196,136,634.\n\nThen add 826,512:\n\n1,196,136,634 +826,512 = ?\n\n1,196,136,634 + 800,000 =1,204,136,634\n\nThen add 26,512: 1,204,136,634 +26,512 =1,204,163,146.\n\nWait this is conflicting. What's the issue here?\n\nAh! Wait a second, the second number is 807,826,512, which is 800,000,000 +7,826,512. So adding that to 389,136,634:\n\n389,136,634 +800,000,000 =1,189,136,634\n\nThen adding the remaining 7,826,512 to this total.\n\nBut 1,189,136,634 +7,826,512 = ?\n\nLet me do this addition properly:\n\n1,189,136,634\n\n+     7,826,512\n\n= ?\n\nAligning the numbers:\n\n1,189,136,634\n\n+     7,826,512\n\n---------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:4 +2=6\n\nTens:3+1=4\n\nHundreds:6+5=11 →1, carryover1\n\nThousands:6+6=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:3+2=5 +1=6\n\nHundred thousands:1+8=9\n\nMillions:9 (from first number's millions place) +7 (second's millions) =16 →6, carryover1\n\nTen millions:8 (first's ten millions) +0 (second has no ten millions) +1 (carryover) =9\n\nHundred millions:1 (first's hundred millions) +0 =1\n\nBillions:1 (from first number's billions place)\n\nSo the result is:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nTen millions:9,\n\nMillions:6,\n\nHundred thousands:9,\n\nTen thousands:6,\n\nThousands:3,\n\nHundreds:1,\n\nTens:4,\n\nOnes:6.\n\nWhich is 1,196,963,146.\n\nAh! Now I see. In the first digit-by-digit breakdown, I had a mistake in the millions place. The first number's millions digit is 9?\n\nWait wait, first number is 389,136,634. Let me break it down properly:\n\n389,136,634:\n\nBreaking down each digit from the right:\n\nPosition1 (ones):4\n\nPosition2 (tens):3\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1\n\nPosition7 (millions):3\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):8\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3\n\nWait the millions digit (position7) is 3, not 9. But in the alternative addition, when adding 389,136,634 +807,826,512, the millions place calculation was 9 (from first number's millions?) +7 (second's millions digit is7). Wait where did the 9 come from?\n\nAh, here's the mistake. In the first number, the millions digit is actually the seventh digit from the right. Let me see:\n\nThe first number is 389,136,634.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n3 (hundred millions),\n\n8 (ten millions),\n\n9 (millions),\n\n1 (hundred thousands),\n\n3 (ten thousands),\n\n6 (thousands),\n\n6 (hundreds),\n\n3 (tens),\n\n4 (ones).\n\nWait, that's incorrect. Wait commas are placed every three digits from the right.\n\n389,136,634 is divided as 389,136,634 → 389 (millions), 136 (thousands), 634.\n\nWait commas are placed as follows:\n\nThe number is 389,136,634, which is 389 million, 136 thousand, 634.\n\nSo breaking down:\n\n3 (hundred millions),\n\n8 (ten millions),\n\n9 (millions),\n\n1 (hundred thousands),\n\n3 (ten thousands),\n\n6 (thousands),\n\n6 (hundreds),\n\n3 (tens),\n\n4 (ones).\n\nTherefore, the millions digit is the third digit from the left, which is 9 (the millions place is the third digit in 389, so 9 in the millions place).\n\nAh! There's the key mistake. Earlier, in the first digit-by-digit analysis, I incorrectly considered the millions digit as 3, but in reality, it's 9.\n\nSo this changes everything. Let's redo the digit-by-digit addition correctly.\n\nFirst number:389,136,634\n\nDigits from right (position1 to position9):\n\nPosition1:4 (ones),\n\nPosition2:3 (tens),\n\nPosition3:6 (hundreds),\n\nPosition4:6 (thousands),\n\nPosition5:3 (ten thousands),\n\nPosition6:1 (hundred thousands),\n\nPosition7:9 (millions),\n\nPosition8:8 (ten millions),\n\nPosition9:3 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number:807,826,512\n\nDigits from right:\n\nPosition1:2,\n\nPosition2:1,\n\nPosition3:5,\n\nPosition4:6,\n\nPosition5:2,\n\nPosition6:8,\n\nPosition7:7 (millions),\n\nPosition8:0,\n\nPosition9:8.\n\nNow adding each position:\n\n1. Position1:4+2=6\n\n2. Position2:3+1=4\n\n3. Position3:6+5=11 →1, carryover1\n\n4. Position4:6+6=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1\n\n5. Position5:3+2=5 +1=6\n\n6. Position6:1+8=9\n\n7. Position7:9 (first's millions) +7 (second's millions)=16 →6, carryover1\n\n8. Position8:8 +0 +1 (carryover)=9\n\n9. Position9:3+8=11 →1, carryover1\n\n10. Position10: carryover1.\n\nNow the digits are:\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:1,\n\nPosition8:9,\n\nPosition7:6,\n\nPosition6:9,\n\nPosition5:6,\n\nPosition4:3,\n\nPosition3:1,\n\nPosition2:4,\n\nPosition1:6.\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\n9 (ten millions),\n\n6 (millions),\n\n9 (hundred thousands),\n\n6 (ten thousands),\n\n3 (thousands),\n\n1 (hundreds),\n\n4 (tens),\n\n6 (ones).\n\nThus the total is 1,196,963,146, which matches the alternative method.\n\nThe initial mistake in the first digit-by-digit breakdown was incorrectly identifying the millions digit of the first number as 3 instead of 9. That's why the confusion arose.\n\nSo the correct answer is 1,196,963,146.\n</think>.\n\n# Question:.\nWhat is the sum of 389136634 and 807826512?.\n# Reasoning:.\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: 4 + 2 = 6.\n2. The tens place: 3 + 1 = 4.\n3. The hundreds place: 6 + 5 = 11 → Write 1, carry over 1.\n4. The thousands place: 6 + 6 + 1 = 13 → Write 3, carry over 1.\n5. The ten thousands place: 3 + 2 + 1 = 6.\n6. The hundred thousands place: 1 + 8 = 9.\n7. The millions place: 9 + 7 = 16 → Write 6, carry over 1.\n8. The ten millions place: 8 + 0 + 1 = 9.\n9. The hundred millions place: 3 +